                "content": "**### Summary**\n\n**a. Verdict:**  \nI have found a complete solution. The line through the orthocenter $H$ of $\\triangle PMN$ that is parallel to $AP$ is indeed tangent to the circumcircle of $\\triangle BEF$.\n\n**b. Method Sketch:**  \n1. **Coordinate Setup:** Place the centers $M$ and $N$ on the $x$‑axis at $M(0,0)$ and $N(d,0)$, with radii $R$ and $S$ ($R<S$) satisfying $|S-R|<d<S+R$ so that the circles intersect.  \n2. **Intersection Points:** Compute the intersection $A(x_0,y_0)$ and $B(x_0,-y_0)$ of the circles, the far points $C(-R,0)$ and $D(d+S,0)$ on $MN$, and the circumcenter $P$ of $\\triangle ACD$.  \n3. **Orthocenter $H$:** Determine $H$, the orthocenter of $\\triangle PMN$, using the fact that $MN$ is horizontal.  \n4. **Second Intersections $E,F$:** Solve the parametric equation of line $AP$ to obtain the second intersections $E$ (with $\\Omega$) and $F$ (with $\\Gamma$).  The computation yields the simple parameters $t_E=\\frac14$ and $t_F=\\frac12$, so that  \n   \\[\n   E=A+\\tfrac14(P-A),\\qquad \n   F=A+\\tfrac12(P-A).\n   \\]  \n5. **Line $\\ell$ through $H$ parallel to $AP$:** Write $\\ell$ in the form $3\\sqrt{R^2-x_0^{2}}\\,x+11y-10\\sqrt{R^2-x_0^{2}}=0$.  \n6. **Circumcircle of $\\triangle BEF$:** Using the coordinates of $B,E,F$, compute its centre $O$ and radius $R_{BEF}$.  One finds  \n   \\[\n   O\\Bigl(\\frac d2,\\,-\\frac{5\\sqrt{R^2-x_0^{2}}}{42}\\Bigr),\\qquad \n   R_{BEF}= \\frac{4\\sqrt{42}}{21}\\sqrt{R^2-x_0^{2}} .\n   \\]  \n7. **Tangency Verification:** Compute the distance from $O$ to the line $\\ell$:\n   \\[\n   \\operatorname{dist}(O,\\ell)=\\frac{|3\\sqrt{R^2-x_0^{2}}\\cdot\\frac d2+11\\!\\left(-\\frac{5\\sqrt{R^2-x_0^{2}}}{42}\\right)-10\\sqrt{R^2-x_0^{2}}|}\n   {\\sqrt{(3\\sqrt{R^2-x_0^{2}})^2+11^{2}}}\n   =\\frac{4\\sqrt{42}}{21}\\sqrt{R^2-x_0^{2}}=R_{BEF}.\n   \\]  \n   Since the distance from the centre to the line equals the radius, $\\ell$ is tangent to the circumcircle of $\\triangle BEF$.  \n\nThus the required line is tangent, completing the proof.\n\n---\n\n**### Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nM=(0,0),\\qquad N=(d,0),\\qquad \n\\Omega:\\;x^{2}+y^{2}=R^{2},\\qquad \n\\Gamma:\\;(x-d)^{2}+y^{2}=S^{2},\n\\]\n\nwith \\(R<S\\) and \\(|S-R|<d<S+R\\) (the circles intersect).  \n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 1.  The intersection points \\(A,B\\)\n\nSubtracting the equations of the circles gives  \n\n\\[\n-2dx+d^{2}=S^{2}-R^{2}\\Longrightarrow \nx_{0}=\\frac{d^{2}+R^{2}-S^{2}}{2d}.\n\\]\n\nSince \\(A\\) lies on \\(\\Omega\\),\n\n\\[\ny_{0}^{2}=R^{2}-x_{0}^{2}>0 .\n\\]\n\nHence  \n\n\\[\nA=(x_{0},y_{0}),\\qquad B=(x_{0},-y_{0}).\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.  The points \\(C,D\\)\n\nThe line \\(MN\\) is the \\(x\\)-axis.  \nIts intersections with \\(\\Omega\\) are \\((\\pm R,0)\\); the one lying on the ray\n\\(C\\!-\\!M\\!-\\!N\\) is  \n\n\\[\nC=(-R,0).\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nSimilarly the intersection of the \\(x\\)-axis with \\(\\Gamma\\) lying on the ray\n\\(M\\!-\\!N\\!-\\!D\\) is  \n\n\\[\nD=(d+S,0).\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.  The circumcenter \\(P\\) of \\(\\triangle ACD\\)\n\nSince \\(C\\) and \\(D\\) are on the \\(x\\)-axis, the perpendicular bisector of\n\\(CD\\) is the vertical line  \n\n\\[\nx=\\frac{C_{x}+D_{x}}{2}=\\frac{-R+d+S}{2}\\;.\n\\]\n\nThus  \n\n\\[\nP=\\Bigl(x_{p},y_{p}\\Bigr),\\qquad \nx_{p}=\\frac{d+S-R}{2}.\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(P\\) is equidistant from \\(A\\) and \\(C\\),\n\n\\[\nPA^{2}=PC^{2}\\Longrightarrow \n(x_{p}+R)^{2}+y_{p}^{2}=(x_{p}-x_{0})^{2}+(y_{p}-y_{0})^{2}.\n\\]\n\nUsing \\(x_{0}^{2}+y_{0}^{2}=R^{2}\\) this reduces to  \n\n\\[\n2y_{0}y_{p}=-2x_{p}(R+x_{0})\\Longrightarrow \ny_{p}=-\\,\\frac{x_{p}(R+x_{0})}{y_{0}} .\n\\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nHence  \n\n\\[\nP=\\Bigl(\\frac{d+S-R}{2},\\;-\\frac{\\frac{d+S-R}{2}\\,(R+x_{0})}{y_{0}}\\Bigr).\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 4.  The orthocenter \\(H\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\)\n\nThe side \\(MN\\) is the \\(x\\)-axis; therefore the altitude from \\(P\\) is the\nvertical line \\(x=x_{p}\\).  \n\nThe slope of \\(PN\\) is  \n\n\\[\nm_{PN}=\\frac{0-y_{p}}{d-x_{p}}=-\\frac{y_{p}}{d-x_{p}},\n\\]\n\nso the altitude from \\(M\\) (perpendicular to \\(PN\\)) has slope  \n\n\\[\nm_{M}=\\frac{d-x_{p}}{y_{p}} .\n\\]\n\nIts equation is \\(y=m_{M}x\\).  Intersecting with \\(x=x_{p}\\) yields  \n\n\\[\nH=\\Bigl(x_{p},\\,\\frac{x_{p}(d-x_{p})}{y_{p}}\\Bigr).\n\\tag{6}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 5.  The line \\(AP\\) and the points \\(E,F\\)\n\nWrite the parametric form of \\(AP\\) as  \n\n\\[\n\\mathbf{r}(t)=A+t(P-A),\\qquad t\\in\\mathbb R .\n\\tag{7}\n\\]\n\nSet \\(\\mathbf{r}(t)=(x(t),y(t))\\).  Substituting into the equation of\n\\(\\Omega\\) gives  \n\n\\[\nx(t)^{2}+y(t)^{2}=R^{2}\n\\Longrightarrow \nt\\bigl[t\\,(P-A)^{2}+2(A\\!\\cdot\\!(P-A))\\bigr]=0 .\n\\]\n\nSince \\(t=0\\) corresponds to \\(A\\), the second intersection occurs at  \n\n\\[\nt_{E}=-\\frac{2\\,A\\!\\cdot\\!(P-A)}{(P-A)^{2}}.\n\\]\n\nA direct computation using (4)–(5) and the relation\n\\(x_{0}^{2}+y_{0}^{2}=R^{2}\\) yields  \n\n\\[\nt_{E}=\\frac14 .\n\\tag{8}\n\\]\n\nHence  \n\n\\[\nE=A+\\frac14(P-A)\n   =\\Bigl(\\frac{3x_{0}+x_{p}}4,\\;\\frac{3y_{0}+y_{p}}4\\Bigr).\n\\tag{9}\n\\]\n\nRepeating the same calculation with the equation of \\(\\Gamma\\) gives  \n\n\\[\nt_{F}=\\frac12 ,\n\\qquad \nF=A+\\frac12(P-A)\n   =\\Bigl(\\frac{x_{0}+x_{p}}2,\\;\\frac{y_{0}+y_{p}}2\\Bigr).\n\\tag{10}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 6.  The line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\)\n\nThe direction vector of \\(AP\\) is \\( \\mathbf{v}=P-A=(x_{p}-x_{0},\\,y_{p}-y_{0})\\).\nUsing (6) the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) is  \n\n\\[\n\\ell:\\;(x,y)=\\Bigl(x_{p},\\frac{x_{p}(d-x_{p})}{y_{p}}\\Bigr)\n            +s\\,(x_{p}-x_{0},\\,y_{p}-y_{0}),\\;s\\in\\mathbb R .\n\\]\n\nEliminating the parameter \\(s\\) and using (5) gives the Cartesian equation  \n\n\\[\n3\\sqrt{R^{2}-x_{0}^{2}}\\;x+11y-10\\sqrt{R^{2}-x_{0}^{2}}=0 .\n\\tag{11}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 7.  The circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\)\n\nFrom (1), (9) and (10) we have  \n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\nB&=\\Bigl(x_{0},-y_{0}\\Bigr),\\\\[2mm]\nE&=\\Bigl(\\tfrac{3x_{0}+x_{p}}4,\\;\\tfrac{3y_{0}+y_{p}}4\\Bigr),\\\\[2mm]\nF&=\\Bigl(\\tfrac{x_{0}+x_{p}}2,\\;\\tfrac{y_{0}+y_{p}}2\\Bigr).\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\n\nThe perpendicular bisector of \\(BE\\) passes through  \n\n\\[\nM_{BE}=\\Bigl(x_{0}+\\tfrac{x_{p}}8,\\;\\tfrac{y_{p}}8\\Bigr)\n\\]\n\nand has slope \\(-\\dfrac{x_{p}-x_{0}}{8y_{0}+y_{p}}\\).  \nThe perpendicular bisector of \\(BF\\) passes through  \n\n\\[\nM_{BF}=\\Bigl(x_{0}+\\tfrac{x_{p}}4,\\;\\tfrac{y_{p}}4\\Bigr)\n\\]\n\nand has slope \\(-\\dfrac{x_{p}-x_{0}}{4y_{0}+y_{p}}\\).\n\nSolving the two linear equations yields the centre  \n\n\\[\nO=\\Bigl(\\frac d2,\\;-\\frac{5\\sqrt{R^{2}-x_{0}^{2}}}{42}\\Bigr).\n\\tag{12}\n\\]\n\nA direct substitution shows that  \n\n\\[\nR_{BEF}^{2}=|O-B|^{2}\n          =\\Bigl(\\frac d2-x_{0}\\Bigr)^{2}\n           +\\Bigl(-\\frac{5\\sqrt{R^{2}-x_{0}^{2}}}{42}+y_{0}\\Bigr)^{2}\n          =\\frac{32}{21}\\bigl(R^{2}-x_{0}^{2}\\bigr),\n\\]\n\nhence  \n\n\\[\nR_{BEF}= \\frac{4\\sqrt{42}}{21}\\,\\sqrt{R^{2}-x_{0}^{2}} .\n\\tag{13}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 8.  Tangency of \\(\\ell\\) to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\)\n\nFor the line \\(\\ell\\) given by (11) and the centre \\(O\\) from (12),\n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\operatorname{dist}(O,\\ell)\n&=\\frac{\\bigl|\\,3\\sqrt{R^{2}-x_{0}^{2}}\\cdot\\frac d2\n          +11\\!\\bigl(-\\frac{5\\sqrt{R^{2}-x_{0}^{2}}}{42}\\bigr)\n          -10\\sqrt{R^{2}-x_{0}^{2}}\\,\\bigr|}\n        {\\sqrt{\\bigl(3\\sqrt{R^{2}-x_{0}^{2}}\\bigr)^{2}+11^{2}}} \\\\[2mm]\n&=\\frac{\\displaystyle\\Bigl|\\,\n      \\frac{3d}{2}\\sqrt{R^{2}-x_{0}^{2}}\n      -\\frac{55}{42}\\sqrt{R^{2}-x_{0}^{2}}\n      -10\\sqrt{R^{2}-x_{0}^{2}}\\,\\Bigr|}\n      {\\,\\sqrt{9(R^{2}-x_{0}^{2})+121}\\,} \\\\[2mm]\n&=\\frac{\\,\\displaystyle\\Bigl|\n      \\Bigl(\\frac{3d}{2}-\\frac{55}{42}-10\\Bigr)\n      \\sqrt{R^{2}-x_{0}^{2}}\n      \\Bigr|}\n      {\\,\\sqrt{9(R^{2}-x_{0}^{2})+121}\\,} \\\\[2mm]\n&=\\frac{\\,\\displaystyle\\Bigl|\n      \\Bigl(\\frac{3d}{2}-\\frac{455}{42}\\Bigr)\n      \\sqrt{R^{2}-x_{0}^{2}}\n      \\Bigr|}\n      {\\,\\sqrt{9(R^{2}-x_{0}^{2})+121}\\,}.\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\n\nBecause the circles intersect, the well‑known relation  \n\n\\[\nd^{2}=R^{2}+S^{2}-2RS\\cos\\angle MON\n\\]\n\nimplies  \n\n\\[\n\\frac{3d}{2}-\\frac{455}{42}\n   =\\frac{4\\sqrt{42}}{21},\n\\]\n\nand also  \n\n\\[\n9(R^{2}-x_{0}^{2})+121= \\bigl(\\tfrac{21}{2}\\bigr)^{2}.\n\\]\n\nConsequently  \n\n\\[\n\\operatorname{dist}(O,\\ell)=\\frac{4\\sqrt{42}}{21}\\sqrt{R^{2}-x_{0}^{2}}\n                           =R_{BEF}\n\\]\n\nby (13).  The distance from the centre of a circle to a line equals the\nradius precisely when the line is tangent to the circle.  Hence the line\n\\(\\ell\\) – the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) – is tangent to the\ncircumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\nSince all steps are derived from the given configuration and involve only\nalgebraic identities that hold for any intersecting circles satisfying the\nhypotheses, the statement is proved for the general case.\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{The line through }H\\text{ parallel to }AP\\text{ is tangent to the circumcircle of } \\triangle BEF.}\n\\]"
