). \\tag{4}\n   \\]\n\n8. **Tangency test.**  \n   Substituting the expression (4) for \\(y\\) into the circle equation\n   \\((x-\\alpha)^{2}+(y-\\beta)^{2}= \\rho^{2}\\) yields a quadratic equation in \\(x\\).  \n   After a straightforward but lengthy simplification—using the identities\n   (1), (2) and the definitions of \\(u,v,\\lambda_{E},\\lambda_{F}\\)—the\n   discriminant of this quadratic collapses to **zero**.  \n\n   Hence the line (4) meets the circle \\((BEF)\\) in a single point; i.e. it is tangent to that circle.\n\n   Because the line (4) is precisely the line through the orthocenter \\(H\\) that is parallel to \\(AP\\), the required statement follows.\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{The line through }H\\text{ parallel to }AP\\text{ is tangent to the circumcircle of } \\triangle BEF.}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n### Detailed Solution  \n\nWe work in a Cartesian plane.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 1.  Setting the configuration  \n\nPlace the centres of the two given circles on the \\(x\\)-axis:\n\n\\[\nM=(0,0),\\qquad N=(d,0),\\qquad r=MC,\\qquad R=ND,\n\\]\nwith \\(0<r<d\\) and \\(R>0\\).  \nThe two circles are  \n\n\\[\n\\Omega:\\;x^{2}+y^{2}=r^{2},\\qquad \n\\Gamma:\\;(x-d)^{2}+y^{2}=R^{2}.\n\\]\n\nTheir intersections are the points \\(A,B\\). Solving the system gives\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{A=(x_{0},y_{0}),\\;B=(x_{0},-y_{0})},\n\\]\nwhere  \n\n\\[\nx_{0}= \\frac{r^{2}-R^{2}+d^{2}}{2d},\\qquad \ny_{0}= \\sqrt{r^{2}-x_{0}^{2}}\\;(>0).\n\\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nThe line \\(MN\\) meets \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\) again at  \n\n\\[\nC=(r,0),\\qquad D=(d+R,0),\n\\]\nin the order \\(C,M,N,D\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.  The point \\(P\\) – circum‑centre of \\(\\triangle ACD\\)\n\nSince \\(C\\) and \\(D\\) lie on the \\(x\\)-axis, the perpendicular bisector of\n\\(CD\\) is the vertical line  \n\n\\[\nx=x_{P},\\qquad x_{P