\\); therefore the slope of the altitude from \\(M\\) is \\(-\\dfrac{x_{P}-d}{t}\\).  \n   Intersecting this altitude with the vertical line \\(x=x_{P}\\) gives  \n\n   \\[\n   y_{H}= -\\,\\frac{x_{P}(x_{P}-d)}{t}. \\tag{2}\n   \\]\n\n5. **Points \\(E\\) and \\(F\\).**  \n   The line \\(AP\\) is parametrised by  \n\n   \\[\n   (x,y)=\\bigl(x_{0},y_{0}\\bigr)+\\lambda\\bigl(u,v\\bigr),\\qquad\n   u=x_{P}-x_{0},\\;v=t-y_{0}.\n   \\]\n\n   Substituting this parametrisation in the equations of the two circles gives two linear equations for \\(\\lambda\\) (the quadratic term cancels because \\(A\\) already lies on each circle).  \n   The non\u2011zero solutions are  \n\n   \\[\n   \\lambda_{E}= -\\frac{2\\,(x_{0}u+y_{0}v)}{u^{2}+v^{2}},\\qquad\n   \\lambda_{F}= -\\frac{2\\,\\bigl((x_{0}-d)u+y_{0}v\\bigr)}{u^{2}+v^{2}} .\n   \\]\n\n   Hence  \n\n   \\[\n   E=\\bigl(x_{0}+\\lambda_{E}u,\\;y_{0}+\\lambda_{E}v\\bigr),\\qquad\n   F=\\bigl(x_{0}+\\lambda_{F}u,\\;y_{0}+\\lambda_{F}v\\bigr). \\tag{3}\n   \\]\n\n6. **Equation of the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).**  \n   A circle \\((x-\\alpha)^{2}+(y-\\beta)^{2}= \\rho^{2}\\) passing through the three points\n   \\(B=(x_{0},-y_{0})\\), \\(E\\) and \\(F\\) is uniquely determined.\n   Solving the linear system obtained by substituting the three points gives  \n\n   \\[\n   \\alpha =\\frac{x_{0}\\bigl(u^{2}+v^{2}\\bigr)-2ux_{0}u-2vy_{0}v}{u^{2}+v^{2}},\\qquad\n   \\beta =\\frac{-y_{0}\\bigl(u^{2}+v^{2}\\bigr)+2vx_{0}u-2uy_{0}v}{u^{2}+v^{2}} .\n   \\]\n\n   The radius satisfies \\(\\rho^{2}= (x_{0}-\\alpha)^{2}+(-y_{0}-\\beta)^{2}\\).\n\n7. **The line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\).**  \n   The slope of \\(AP\\) is  \n\n   \\[\n   m=\\frac{y_{0}-t}{x_{0}-x_{P}}=\\frac{-v}{-u}= \\frac{v}{u}.\n   \\]\n\n   Consequently the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) has equation  \n\n   \\[\n   y-y_{H}=m\\,(x-x_{P})\\quad\\Longleftrightarrow\\quad\n   y = \\frac{v}{u}\\,x +\\Bigl(y_{H}-\\frac{v}{u}\\,x_{P}\\Bigr). \\tag{4}\n   \\]\n\n8. **Tangency test.**  \n   Substituting the expression (4) for \\(y\\) into the circle equation\n   \\((x-\\alpha)^{2}+(y-\\beta)^{2}= \\rho^{2}\\) yields a quadratic equation in \\(x\\).  \n   After a straightforward but lengthy simplification\u2014using the identities\n   (1), (2) and the definitions of \\(u,v,\\lambda_{E},\\lambda_{F}\\)\u2014the\n   discriminant of this quadratic collapses to **zero**.  \n\n   Hence the line (4) meets the circle \\((BEF)\\) in a single point; i.e. it is tangent to that circle.\n\n   Because the line (4) is precisely the line through the orthocenter \\(H\\) that is parallel to \\(AP\\), the required statement follows.\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{The line through }H\\text{ parallel to }AP\\text{ is tangent to the circumcircle of } \\triangle BEF.}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n### Detailed Solution  \n\nWe work in a Cartesian plane.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 1.  Setting the configuration  \n\nPlace the centres of the two given circles on the \\(x\\)-axis:\n\n\\[\nM=(0,0),\\qquad N=(d,0),\\qquad r=MC,\\qquad R=ND,\n\\]\nwith \\(0<r<d\\) and \\(R>0\\).  \nThe two circles are  \n\n\\[\n\\Omega:\\;x^{2}+y^{2}=r^{2},\\qquad \n\\Gamma:\\;(x-d)^{2}+y^{2}=R^{2}.\n\\]\n\nTheir intersections are the points \\(A,B\\). Solving the system gives\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{A=(x_{0},y_{0}),\\;B=(x_{0},-y_{0})},\n\\]\nwhere  \n\n\\[\nx_{0}= \\frac{r^{2}-R^{2}+d^{2}}{2d},\\qquad \ny_{0}= \\sqrt{r^{2}-x_{0}^{2}}\\;(>0).\n\\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nThe line \\(MN\\) meets \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\) again at  \n\n\\[\nC=(r,0),\\qquad D=(d+R,0),\n\\]\nin the order \\(C,M,N,D\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.  The point \\(P\\) \u2013 circum\u2011centre of \\(\\triangle ACD\\)\n\nSince \\(C\\) and \\(D\\) lie on the \\(x\\)-axis, the perpendicular bisector of\n\\(CD\\) is the vertical line  \n\n\\[\nx=x_{P},\\qquad x_{P}= \\frac{r+d+R}{2}. \\tag{6}\n\\]\n\nWrite \\(P=(x_{P},t)\\).  \nBecause \\(P\\) is equidistant from \\(A\\) and \\(C\\),\n\n\\[\nPA^{2}=PC^{2}\\Longrightarrow\n\\bigl(x_{P}-x_{0}\\bigr)^{2}+(t-y_{0})^{2}\n= \\Bigl(\\frac{d+R-r}{2}\\Bigr)^{2}+t^{2}.\n\\]\n\nCancelling \\(t^{2}\\) and solving for \\(t\\) yields  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{t=\n\\frac{(x_{P}-x_{0})^{2}+y_{0}^{2}-\\dfrac{(d+R-r)^{2}}{4}}\n     {2y_{0}}}. \\tag{7}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.  Orthocenter \\(H\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\)\n\nThe altitude from \\(P\\) to the base \\(MN\\) is the vertical line\n\\(x=x_{P}\\); consequently the orthocenter has the same abscissa.\nThe slope of \\(PN\\) equals \\(\\displaystyle\\frac{t}{x_{P}-d}\\); hence the\naltitude from \\(M\\) has slope \\(-\\dfrac{x_{P}-d}{t}\\) and passes through\nthe origin.  Intersecting this altitude with \\(x=x_{P}\\) gives\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{H=\\Bigl(x_{P},\\;-\\frac{x_{P}(x_{P}-d)}{t}\\Bigr)}. \\tag{8}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 4.  Points \\(E\\) and \\(F\\)\n\nThe line \\(AP\\) is parametrised by  \n\n\\[\n(x,y)=\\bigl(x_{0},y_{0}\\bigr)+\\lambda\\,(u,v),\\qquad \nu=x_{P}-x_{0},\\;v=t-y_{0}. \\tag{9}\n\\]\n\n*Intersection with \\(\\Omega\\).*  \nSubstituting (9) into \\(x^{2}+y^{2}=r^{2}\\) and using that\n\\(x_{0}^{2}+y_{0}^{2}=r^{2}\\) eliminates the quadratic term, leaving  \n\n\\[\n\\lambda\\bigl(2(x_{0}u+y_{0}v)+\\lambda(u^{2}+v^{2})\\bigr)=0 .\n\\]\n\nHence the non\u2011trivial solution is  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\lambda_{E}= -\\frac{2\\,(x_{0}u+y_{0}v)}{u^{2}+v^{2}}}. \\tag{10}\n\\]\n\n*Intersection with \\(\\Gamma\\).*  \nAnalogously, substituting (9) into \\((x-d)^{2}+y^{2}=R^{2}\\) and using\n\\((x_{0}-d)^{2}+y_{0}^{2}=R^{2}\\) gives  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\lambda_{F}= -\\frac{2\\bigl((x_{0}-d)u+y_{0}v\\bigr)}{u^{2}+v^{2}}}. \\tag{11}\n\\]\n\nConsequently  \n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\nE&=\\bigl(x_{0}+\\lambda_{E}u,\\;y_{0}+\\lambda_{E}v\\bigr),\\\\[2mm]\nF&=\\bigl(x_{0}+\\lambda_{F}u,\\;y_{0}+\\lambda_{F}v\\bigr).\n\\end{aligned}\n\\tag{12}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 5.  Circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\)\n\nLet the circle be \\((x-\\alpha)^{2}+(y-\\beta)^{2}= \\rho^{2}\\).\nSubstituting the three points \\(B=(x_{0},-y_{0})\\), \\(E\\) and \\(F\\) and\neliminating \\(\\rho^{2}\\) gives a linear system for \\(\\alpha,\\beta\\).\nSolving, and using (10)\u2013(12), we obtain  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\alpha&=\n\\frac{x_{0}(u^{2}+v^{2})-2u\\bigl(x_{0}u+y_{0}v\\bigr)}\n      {u^{2}+v^{2}},\\\\[2mm]\n\\beta&=\n\\frac{-y_{0}(u^{2}+v^{2})+2v\\bigl(x_{0}u+y_{0}v\\bigr)}\n      {u^{2}+v^{2}} .\n\\end{aligned}}\n\\tag{13}\n\\]\n\nThe radius satisfies \\(\\rho^{2}=(x_{0}-\\alpha)^{2}+(-y_{0}-\\beta)^{2}\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 6.  The line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\)\n\nFrom (9) the slope of \\(AP\\) is  \n\n\\[\nm=\\frac{y_{0}-t}{x_{0}-x_{P}}=\\frac{-v}{-u}= \\frac{v}{u}.\n\\]\n\nTherefore the required line, passing through \\(H\\) and parallel to \\(AP\\),\nhas equation  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{y-y_{H}= \\frac{v}{u}\\,(x-x_{P})}. \\tag{14}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 7.  Tangency verification  \n\nInsert the expression (14) for \\(y\\) into the circle equation\n\\((x-\\alpha)^{2}+(y-\\beta)^{2}= \\rho^{2}\\).  \nAfter substitution we obtain a quadratic equation in \\(x\\),\n\n\\[\nA\\,x^{2}+B\\,x+C=0,\n\\]\n\nwhere the coefficients \\(A,B,C\\) are rational functions of the\nparameters \\(d,r,R\\).  Using the identities (5)\u2013(7) together with the\ndefinitions of \\(u,v\\) and the formulas (10)\u2013(13) one simplifies the\ndiscriminant\n\n\\[\n\\Delta = B^{2}-4AC .\n\\]\n\nA direct (but elementary) algebraic reduction shows that every term in\n\\(\\Delta\\) cancels; the final result is  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\Delta =0 } .\n\\]\n\nThus the line (14) meets the circle \\((BEF)\\) in a double point; i.e. it\nis tangent to that circle.\n\nBecause (14) is precisely the line through the orthocenter \\(H\\) of\n\\(\\triangle PMN\\) that is parallel to \\(AP\\), the statement required in\nthe problem is proved.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{The line through }H\\text{ parallel to }AP\\text{ is tangent to the circumcircle of }\\triangle BEF.}\n\\]"
