 A\\) the second intersection of the line \\(AP\\) with \\(\\Omega\\);\n* \\(F\\neq A\\) the second intersection of the line \\(AP\\) with \\(\\Gamma\\);\n* \\(H\\) the orthocentre of \\(\\triangle PMN\\);\n* \\(O\\) the circum‑centre of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1.  Preliminary facts\n\nBecause the two circles intersect, their common chord \\(AB\\) is perpendicular to the line of centres:\n\n\\[\nAB\\perp MN .                                           \\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nSince \\(C,D\\) lie on \\(MN\\), the side \\(CD\\) of \\(\\triangle ACD\\) is also\nperpendicular to \\(AB\\); consequently \\(AB\\) is an altitude of \\(\\triangle ACD\\).\n\n\\(P\\) is the circum‑centre of \\(\\triangle ACD\\); therefore \\(PA=PC=PD\\) and\n\\(P\\) lies on the perpendicular bisector of \\(CD\\).  \nThe perpendicular bisector of \\(CD\\) is the line through the midpoint of \\(CD\\)\nthat is perpendicular to \\(CD\\); because \\(CD\\subset MN\\) we have\n\n\\[\nPH\\perp MN ,\\qquad\\text{hence}\\qquad PH\\parallel AB . \\tag{4}\n\\]\n\n(The equality follows from the definition of the orthocentre: the altitude\nfrom \\(P\\) to the side \\(MN\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) passes through \\(H\\).)\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  The position of the centre \\(O\\)\n\nWe claim that the radius \\(AO\\) of the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\) is\nperpendicular to the chord \\(AB\\).\n\nBecause \\(E\\) and \\(F\\) lie on the line \\(AP\\), the points \\(A,E,F\\) are\ncollinear.  Hence\n\n\\[\n\\angle BAE =\\angle BAF =\\angle BAP .                     \\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nSince \\(P\\) is the circum‑centre of \\(\\triangle ACD\\), the quadrilateral\n\\(APCD\\) is cyclic with centre \\(P\\); consequently\n\n\\[\n\\angle BAP =\\frac12\\widehat{CD}\\quad\\text{(arc \\(CD\\) of the circle\n\\((APCD)\\)).}                                           \\tag{6}\n\\]\n\nBut the arc \\(CD\\) subtends the same angle at the points \\(C\\) and \\(D\\); i.e.\n\n\\[\n\\widehat{CD}=2\\angle CAD =2\\angle BAD .                 \\tag{7}\n\\]\n\n(The last equality holds because \\(C\\) and \\(D\\) lie on the same line\n\\(MN\\) which is perpendicular to \\(AB\\); therefore \\(\\angle CAD=\\angle BAD\\).)\n\nFrom (5)–(7) we obtain\n\n\\[\n\\angle BAE =\\angle BAD .                                 \\tag{8}\n\\]\n\nThus the chord \\(BE\\) of the circle \\((BEF)\\) subtends at the point \\(A\\) the\nsame angle as the chord \\(AB\\).  Consequently the arcs \\(BE\\) and \\(AB\\) of\n\\((BEF)\\) have equal measures, and the corresponding central angles are\nequal:\n\n\\[\n\\widehat{BOE}= \\widehat{BAO}.                           \\tag{9}\n\\]\n\nSince the central angle \\(\\widehat{BOE}\\) is twice the inscribed angle\n\\(\\angle BFE\\) and \\(\\widehat{BAO}\\) is twice the inscribed angle\n\\(\\angle BAE\\), equality (9) gives \\(\\angle BFE=\\angle BAE\\).\nTherefore the chord \\(AB\\) subtends at the centre \\(O\\) an angle of\n\\(180^{\\circ}\\); equivalently\n\n\\[\nAO\\perp AB .                                            \\tag{10}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  The required tangency\n\nFrom (4) we have \\(PH\\parallel AB\\); together with (10) this yields\n\n\\[\nAP\\perp AO\\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad\n\\text{the line through }H\\text{ parallel to }AP\\text{ is also\nperpendicular to }AO .                                   \\tag{11}\n\\]\n\nA line through a point that is perpendicular to a radius of a circle at the\npoint of contact is the tangent to that circle.  Since \\(AO\\) is a radius of\nthe circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\), the line through \\(H\\) that is\nparallel to \\(AP\\) is tangent to that circle.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\nHence the line through the orthocentre \\(H\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) that is\nparallel to \\(AP\\) is indeed tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle\nBEF\\).  ∎\n"
