e side MN; its foot is the orthocentre H. Hence PH⊥MN.\",\n      \"description\": \"The foot of the altitude from P onto MN is not the orthocenter H; H is the intersection of all three altitudes. Therefore the statement PH⊥MN is incorrect, and the subsequent parallelism PH∥AB is unfounded.\"\n    }\n  ],\n  \"reasoning\": \"The solution contains several demonstrably false intermediate claims (e.g., S(C)=D, O∈MN, AP⊥AB, and misidentifying the orthocenter as the foot of an altitude). These critical errors invalidate the proof, so the answer does not correctly establish the required result.\",\n  \"verdict\": \"FAIL\"\n}\n\nMake sure JSON is valid and no extra formatting. Ensure location quotes are within 50-200 characters. Let's count approximate length: The location strings are maybe >200? Let's check each location length.\n\nFirst location: \"Since the circles Ω and Γ intersect at the two points A and B, there exists a unique spiral similarity S centred at A that sends Ω onto Γ. The similarity S satisfies S(C)=D, S(M)=N.\" That's about 150-200 characters? Let's count: \"Since the circles Ω and Γ intersect at the two points A and B, there exists a unique spiral similarity S centred at A that sends Ω onto Γ. The similarity S satisfies S(C)=D, S(M)=N.\" Count characters: Let's approximate: \"Since the circles Ω and Γ intersect at the two points A and B, there exists a unique spiral similarity S centred at A that sends Ω onto Γ. The similarity S satisfies S(C)=D, S(M)=N.\" That's maybe around 170 characters. Should be okay.\n\nSecond location: \"Because S is centred at A, the image of any point lies on the ray issued from A through that point; therefore from (2) O∈AP. From (1) we see that S sends the line MN onto itself, hence the image of any point of MN again belongs to MN. In particular, the image of P (which is O) lies on MN; thus O∈MN.\" That's longer, maybe >200. Let's shorten.\n\nWe need location string 50-200 characters. Let's extract a concise excerpt: \"From (