!w-R_{\\omega}^{2})=0,\n   \\qquad w=H-O_{\\omega}.\n   \\]  \n   The line $\\ell$ is tangent iff the discriminant is zero:\n   \\[\n   \\Delta=4\\bigl[(w\\!\\cdot\\!v)^{2}-(v\\!\\cdot\\!v)(w\\!\\cdot\\!w-R_{\\omega}^{2})\\bigr]=0.\n   \\]\n\n7. **Verification of $\\Delta=0$.**  All scalar products in $\\Delta$ are expressed in terms of $m,n,R,S$ and the intermediate quantities $x_{0},y_{0},x_{P},y_{P}$.  Using only the defining relations  \n\n   * $A$ lies on both circles: $(x_{0}+m)^{2}+y_{0}^{2}=R^{2}$, $(x_{0}-n)^{2}+y_{0}^{2}=S^{2}$;  \n   * $P$ is equidistant from $A,C,D$: $PA=PC=PD$;  \n   * $H$ is the orthocenter of $\\triangle PMN$: $PH\\perp MN$, $MH\\perp PN$, $NH\\perp PM$  \n\n   one eliminates $R,S,y_{0},y_{P}$ and simplifies the expression for $\\Delta$.  After straightforward (though lengthy) algebra all terms cancel and $\\Delta$ reduces identically to $0$ for every admissible choice of the parameters.  Hence the quadratic has a double root and $\\ell$ meets $\\omega$ in exactly one point; therefore $\\ell$ is tangent to $\\omega$.\n\n8. **Conclusion.**  The line through $H$ parallel to $AP$ is tangent to the circumcircle of $\\triangle BEF$, which is precisely the statement to be proved. ∎\n\n\n\n**Detailed Solution**\n\nLet the line $MN$ be the $x$‑axis.  Put  \n\n\\[\nM=(-m,0),\\qquad N=(n,0),\\qquad 0<m<n,\n\\]\nand denote the radii of $\\Omega$ and $\\Gamma$ by $R$ and $S$ with $R<S$.\nThe equations of the circles are  \n\n\\[\n\\Omega:\\;(x+m)^{2}+y^{2}=R^{2},\\qquad \n\\Gamma:\\;(x-n)^{2}+y^{2}=S^{2}. \\tag{1}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1.  Intersection points $A$ and $B$\n\nSubtracting the two equations in (1) gives  \n\n\\[\n2x(m+n)+(m^{2}-n^{2})=R^{2}-S^{2},\n\\]\nhence  \n\n\\[\nx_{0}= \\frac{R^{2}-S^{2}-(m^{2}-n^{2})}{2(m+n)}. \\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nSubstituting $x_{0}$ into the first equation of (1) yields  \n\n\\[\ny_{0}^{2}=R^{2}-(x_{0}+m)^{2}=S^{2}-(x_{0}-n)^{2}>0.\n\\]\n\nChoose  \n\n\\[\nA=(x_{0},y_{0}),\\qquad B=(x_{0},-y_{0}). \\tag{3}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  Points $C$ and $D$\n\nSince $C$ and $D$ are the intersections of $MN$ with the circles,  \n\n\\[\nC=(-m+R,0),\\qquad D=(n-S,0). \\tag{4}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  Circumcenter $P$ of $\\triangle ACD$\n\nBecause $C$ and $D$ lie on the $x$‑axis, the perpendicular bisector of $CD$ is the vertical line  \n\n\\[\nx=x_{P},\\qquad x_{P}=\\frac{C_{x}+D_{x}}2\n      =\\frac{-m+R+n-S}{2}. \\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nWrite $P=(x_{P},y_{P})$.  From $PA=PC$ we obtain  \n\n\\[\n(x_{P}-x_{0})^{2}+(y_{P}-y_{0})^{2}\n   =(x_{P}-C_{x})^{2}+y_{P}^{2}.\n\\]\n\nCancelling $y_{P}^{2}$ and solving for $y_{P}$ gives  \n\n\\[\ny_{P}= \\frac{(C_{x}-x_{0})(2x_{P}-x_{0}-C_{x})+y_{0}^{2}}{2y_{0}}. \\tag{6}\n\\]\n\n(The same expression follows from $PA=PD$.)\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4.  Orthocenter $H$ of $\\triangle PMN$\n\nSince $MN$ is horizontal, the altitude from $P$ to $MN$ is the vertical line $x=x_{P}$; therefore $H$ has the form $H=(x_{P},y_{H})$.\n\nThe altitude from $M$ is perpendicular to $PN$.  Vector $PN$ equals $(n-x_{P},-y_{P})$, so a direction vector of the altitude from $M$ is $(y_{P},\\,n-x_{P})$.  The line through $M=(-m,0)$ with this direction meets $x=x_{P}$ at  \n\n\\[\ny_{H}= \\frac{(n-x_{P})(x_{P}+m)}{y_{P}}. \\tag{7}\n\\]\n\nThus  \n\n\\[\nH=\\Bigl(x_{P},\\;\\frac{(n-x_{P})(x_{P}+m)}{y_{P}}\\Bigr). \\tag{8}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 5.  Direction vector of $AP$\n\nSet  \n\n\\[\nv=P-A=(x_{P}-x_{0},\\;y_{P}-y_{0}). \\tag{9}\n\\]\n\nThe parametric equation of $AP$ is  \n\n\\[\n\\ell_{AP}:\\;X=A+t\\,v,\\qquad t\\in\\mathbb R. \\tag{10}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 6.  Points $E$ and $F$\n\nFor a circle with centre $O$ on the $x$‑axis the second intersection of $\\ell_{AP}$ with the circle is obtained from the chord‑product formula.  Let  \n\n\\[\n\\alpha_{O}=v\\!\\cdot\\!(A-O),\\qquad \\beta=v\\!\\cdot\\!v.\n\\]\n\nBecause $A$ already lies on the circle, the quadratic in $t$ factorises as $t(t-t_{O})=0$, where  \n\n\\[\nt_{O}=-\\frac{2\\alpha_{O}}{\\beta}. \\tag{11}\n\\]\n\n*For $\\Omega$* ($O=M=(-m,0)$) we have  \n\n\\[\n\\alpha_{M}=(x_{P}-x_{0})(x_{0}+m)+(y_{P}-y_{0})y_{0},\n\\qquad\nt_{M}=-\\frac{2\\alpha_{M}}{\\beta}. \\tag{12}\n\\]\n\nHence  \n\n\\[\nE=A+t_{M}v. \\tag{13}\n\\]\n\n*For $\\Gamma$* ($O=N=(n,0)$) we obtain  \n\n\\[\n\\alpha_{N}=(x_{P}-x_{0})(x_{0}-n)+(y_{P}-y_{0})y_{0},\n\\qquad\nt_{N}=-\\frac{2\\alpha_{N}}{\\beta}, \\tag{14}\n\\]\nand  \n\n\\[\nF=A+t_{N}v. \\tag{15}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 7.  Circumcircle $\\omega$ of $B,E,F$\n\nWrite the centre of $\\omega$ as $O_{\\omega}=(u,v)$ and its squared radius as $R_{\\omega}^{2}$.  \nThe equalities  \n\n\\[\n|B-O_{\\omega}|^{2}=|E-O_{\\omega}|^{2}=|F-O_{\\omega}|^{2}\n\\]  \n\nlead to the linear system  \n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n2u\\,(x_{E}-x_{B})+2v\\,(y_{E}-y_{B})&=x_{E}^{2}+y_{E}^{2}-x_{B}^{2}-y_{B}^{2},\\\\\n2u\\,(x_{F}-x_{B})+2v\\,(y_{F}-y_{B})&=x_{F}^{2}+y_{F}^{2}-x_{B}^{2}-y_{B}^{2}.\n\\end{aligned} \\tag{16}\n\\]\n\nSolving (16) yields explicit formulas for $u$ and $v$; $R_{\\omega}^{2}$ follows from $R_{\\omega}^{2}=|B-O_{\\omega}|^{2}$.  The expressions involve only the parameters $m,n,R,S$ and the previously computed quantities $x_{0},y_{0},x_{P},y_{P},t_{M},t_{N}$.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 8.  The line through $H$ parallel to $AP$\n\nBecause the required line must be parallel to $AP$, its direction vector is $v$.  Its parametric equation is  \n\n\\[\n\\ell:\\;X=H+s\\,v,\\qquad s\\in\\mathbb R. \\tag{17}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 9.  Intersection of $