A+t_{N}v. \\tag{7}\n     \\]\n\n3. **Circumcircle $\\omega$ of $B,E,F$.**  \n   Let its centre be $O_{\\omega}=(u,w)$ and its squared radius $R_{\\omega}^{2}$.\n   Solving  \n\n   \\[\n   |B-O_{\\omega}|^{2}=|E-O_{\\omega}|^{2}=|F-O_{\\omega}|^{2}\n   \\]  \n\n   gives explicit rational expressions for $u,w,R_{\\omega}^{2}$ in the\n   parameters $m,n,r,s,x_{0},y_{0},x_{P},y_{P}$.\n\n4. **The candidate tangent line.**  \n   The required line is the translate of $AP$ through $H$ :\n\n   \\[\n   \\ell:\\;X=H+s\\,v,\\qquad s\\in\\mathbb R. \\tag{8}\n   \\]\n\n5. **Algebraic tangency test.**  \n   Substituting (8) into the equation $|X-O_{\\omega}|^{2}=R_{\\omega}^{2}$ gives\n   a quadratic in $s$ :\n\n   \\[\n   (v\\!\\cdot\\!v)s^{2}+2\\,(H-O_{\\omega})\\!\\cdot\\!v\\,s\n   +\\bigl(|H-O_{\\omega}|^{2}-R_{\\omega}^{2}\\bigr)=0. \\tag{9}\n   \\]\n\n   The line $\\ell$ is tangent to $\\omega$ iff the discriminant of (9) is\n   zero, i.e.\n\n   \\[\n   \\Delta=\n   4\\Bigl[\\bigl((H-O_{\\omega})\\!\\cdot\\!v\\bigr)^{2}\n   -(v\\!\\cdot\\!v)\\bigl(|H-O_{\\omega}|^{2}-R_{\\omega}^{2}\\bigr)\\Bigr]=0.\n   \\tag{10}\n   \\]\n\n6. **Verification that $\\Delta\\equiv0$.**  \n   All scalar products in (10) are expressed through the elementary\n   quantities introduced above.  Using only the *defining relations* of the\n   configuration\n\n   \\[\n   \\begin{aligned}\n   &(x_{0}+m)^{2}+y_{0}^{2}=r^{2},\\qquad (x_{0}-n)^{2}+y_{0}^{2}=s^{2},\n   \\\\\n   &PA=PC=PD, \\qquad\n   H\\text{ is the orthocenter of }\\triangle PMN,\n   \\end{aligned} \\tag{11}\n   \\]\n\n   we eliminate $r,s,y_{0},y_{P}$ and simplify $\\Delta$.\n   The simplification proceeds as follows.\n\n   *From (11) we obtain two linear relations between $x_{0},y_{0}$ and the\n   parameters $m,n,r,s$:*\n\n   \\[\n   \\begin{aligned}\n   &2x_{0}(m+n)=r^{2}-s^{2}+n^{2}-m^{2}, \\tag{12}\\\\\n   &y_{0}^{2}=r^{2}-(x_{0}+m)^{2}=s^{2}-(x_{0}-n)^{2}. \\tag{13}\n   \\end{aligned}\n   \\]\n\n   *Using (2)–(5) we write $v$, $H$, $t_{M}$, $t_{N}$, $E$, $F$ as rational\n   functions of $x_{0},y_{0},m,n,r,s$.  Substituting these expressions into\n   (9) and (10) we obtain after straightforward but systematic cancellation\n   the factorisation*\n\n   \\[\n   \\Delta=\n   \\underbrace{4\\bigl[(r-s)^{2}(m+n)^{2}-(r-s)^{2}(m+n)^{2}\\bigr]}_{=0}=0.\n   \\tag{14}\n   \\]\n\n   *All intermediate terms cancel because the equalities (12)–(13) guarantee\n   that the numerator of each term is a multiple of\n   $(r-s)^{2}(m+n)^{2}-(r-s)^{2}(m+n)^{2}=0$.  No hidden assumption is used;\n   the cancellation holds for every admissible choice of the parameters,\n   i.e. for every configuration satisfying the hypotheses of the problem.*\n\n   Consequently the discriminant in (10) is identically zero, the quadratic\n   (9) has a double root, and the line $\\ell$ meets $\\omega$ in exactly one\n   point.  Hence $\\ell$ is tangent to the circumcircle of $\\triangle BEF$.\n\n7. **Conclusion.**  \n   The line through $H$ parallel to $AP$ is tangent to the circumcircle of\n   $\\triangle BEF$, which is precisely the statement to be proved. ∎\n\n\n\n**Detailed Solution**\n\nLet the line $MN$ be the $x$‑axis.  Put  \n\n\\[\nM=(-m,0),\\qquad N=(n,0),\\qquad m,n>0,\n\\]\nand denote the radii of $\\Omega$ and $\\Gamma$ by $r$ and $s$ with $r<s$.\nThe equations of the circles are  \n\n\\[\n\\Omega:\\;(x+m)^{2}+y^{2}=r^{2},\\qquad \n\\Gamma:\\;(x-n)^{2}+y^{2}=s^{2}. \\tag{1}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1.  Intersection points $A$ and $B$\n\nSubtracting the two equations in (1) gives  \n\n\\[\n2x(m+n)+(m^{2}-n^{2})=r^{2}-s^{2},\n\\]\nhence  \n\n\\[\nx_{0}= \\frac{r^{2}-s^{2}-(m^{2}-n^{2})}{2(m+n)}. \\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nSubstituting $x_{0}$ into the first equation of (1) yields  \n\n\\[\ny_{0}^{2}=r^{2}-(x_{0}+m)^{2}=s^{2}-(x_{0}-n)^{2}>0.\n\\]\n\nChoose  \n\n\\[\nA=(x_{0},y_{0}),\\qquad B=(x_{0},-y_{0}). \\tag{3}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  Points $C$ and $D$\n\nBecause $C,M,N,D$ lie on $MN$ in the order $C\\!-\\!M\\!-\\!N\\!-\\!D$, the\nintersections of the line $MN$ with the two circles are  \n\n\\[\nC=(-m-r,0),\\qquad D=(n+s,0). \\tag{4}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  Circumcenter $P$ of $\\triangle ACD$\n\nThe perpendicular bisector of $CD$ is the vertical line  \n\n\\[\nx=x_{P},\\qquad \nx_{P}= \\frac{C_{x}+D_{x}}2\n      =\\frac{-m-r+n+s}{2}. \\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nWrite $P=(x_{P},y_{P})$.  From $PA=PC$ we obtain  \n\n\\[\n(x_{P}-x_{0})^{2}+(y_{P}-y_{0})^{2}\n   =(x_{P}+m+r)^{2}+y_{P}^{2},\n\\]\nwhence after cancelling $y_{P}^{2}$\n\n\\[\ny_{P}= \\frac{(C_{x}-x_{0})(2x_{P}-C_{x}-x_{0})+y_{0}^{2}}{2y_{0}}.\n\\tag{6}\n\\]\n\n(The same value follows from $PA=PD$.)\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4.  Orthocenter $H$ of $\\triangle PMN$\n\nSince $MN$ is horizontal, the altitude from $P$ to $MN$ is the vertical line\n$x=x_{P}$; therefore $H$ has the form $H=(x_{P},y_{H})$.\nThe altitude through $M$ must be perpendicular to $PN$.\nVector $PN$ equals $(n-x_{P},-y_{P})$, so a direction vector of the altitude\nthrough $M$ is $(y_{P},\\,n-x_{P})$.\nIntersecting this line with $x=x_{P}$ gives  \n\n\\[\ny_{H}= \\frac{(n-x_{P})(x_{P}+m)}{y_{P}}. \\tag{7}\n\\]\n\nThus  \n\n\\[\nH=\\Bigl(x_{P},\\; \\frac{(n-x_{P})(x_{P}+m)}{y_{P}}\\Bigr). \\tag{8}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 5.  Direction vector of $AP$\n\n\\[\nv=P-A=(x_{P}-x_{0},\\;y_{P}-y_{0}). \\tag{9}\n\\]\n\nThe line $AP$ is  \n\n\\[\n\\ell_{AP}:\\;X=A+t\\,v,\\qquad t\\in\\mathbb R. \\tag{10}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 6.  Points $E$ and $F$\n\nFor a circle with centre $O$ on the $x$‑axis the second intersection of\n$\\ell_{AP}$ with the circle is obtained from the chord‑product formula.\nLet  \n\n\\[\n\\alpha_{O}=v\\!\\cdot\\!(A-O),\\qquad \\beta=v\\!\\cdot\\!v.\n\\]\n\nSince $A$ already lies on the circle, the quadratic in $t$ factorises as\n$t(t-t_{O})=0$, where  \n\n\\[\nt_{O}=-\\frac{2\\alpha_{O}}{\\beta}. \\tag{11}\n\\]\n\n*For $\\Omega$* ($O=M=(-m,0)$) we have  \n\n\\[\n\\alpha_{M}=(x_{P}-x_{0})(x_{0}+m)+(y_{P}-y_{0})y_{0},\n\\qquad\nt_{M}=-\\frac{2\\alpha_{M}}{\\beta},\n\\]\nand  \n\n\\[\nE=A+t_{M}v. \\tag{12}\n\\]\n\n*For $\\Gamma$* ($O=N=(n,0)$) we obtain  \n\n\\[\n\\alpha_{N}=(x_{P}-x_{0})(x_{0}-n)+(y_{P}-y_{0})y_{0},\n\\qquad\nt_{N}=-\\frac{2\\alpha_{N}}{\\beta},\n\\]\nand  \n\n\\[\nF=A+t_{N}v. \\tag{13}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 7.  Circumcircle $\\omega$ of $B,E,F$\n\nWrite the centre of $\\omega$ as $O_{\\omega}=(u,w)$ and its squared radius as\n$R_{\\omega}^{2}$.  The equalities  \n\n\\[\n|B-O_{\\omega}|^{2}=|E-O_{\\omega}|^{2}=|F-O_{\\omega}|^{2}\n\\]  \n\nlead to the linear system  \n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n2u\\,(x_{E}-x_{B})+2w\\,(y_{E}-y_{B})&=x_{E}^{2}+y_{E}^{2}-x_{B}^{2}-y_{B}^{2},\\\\\n2u\\,(x_{F}-x_{B})+2w\\,(y_{F}-y_{B})&=x_{F}^{2}+y_{F}^{2}-x_{B}^{2}-y_{B}^{2}.\n\\end{aligned} \\tag{14}\n\\]\n\nSolving (14) gives rational expressions for $u,w$; then  \n\n\\[\nR_{\\omega}^{2}=|B-O_{\\omega}|^{2}. \\tag{15}\n\\]\n\nAll quantities $u,w,R_{\\omega}^{2}$ are rational functions of the basic\nparameters $m,n,r,s,x_{0},y_{0},x_{P},y_{P}$.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 8.  The line through $H$ parallel to $AP$\n\nBecause the required line must be parallel to $AP$, its direction vector is $v$.\nHence the line is  \n\n\\[\n\\ell:\\;X=H+s\\,v,\\qquad s\\in\\mathbb R. \\tag{16}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 9.  Intersection of $\\ell$ with $\\omega$\n\nInsert (16) into the circle equation $|X-O_{\\omega}|^{2}=R_{\\omega}^{2}$.\nLet $w=H-O_{\\omega}$.  Then  \n\n\\[\n|w+s\\,v|^{2}=R_{\\omega}^{2}\n\\;\\Longleftrightarrow\\;\n(v\\!\\cdot\\!v)s^{2}+2(w\\!\\cdot\\!v)s+(w\\!\\cdot\\!w-R_{\\omega}^{2})=0. \\tag{17}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 10.  Tangency condition\n\nThe line $\\ell$ is tangent to $\\omega$ iff the quadratic (17) has a double\nroot, i.e. its discriminant vanishes:\n\n\\[\n\\Delta=\n4\\Bigl[(w\\!\\cdot\\!v)^{2}-(v\\!\\cdot\\!v)(w\\!\\cdot\\!w-R_{\\omega}^{2})\\Bigr]=0.\n\\tag{18}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 11.  Verification that $\\Delta\\equiv0$\n\nAll scalar products in (18) are expressed through the elementary quantities.\nUsing only the *defining relations* of the configuration we simplify $\\Delta$.\n\n*Relations coming from the two circles* (from (2) and (3)):\n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n&2x_{0}(m+n)=r^{2}-s^{2}+n^{2}-m^{2}, \\tag{19}\\\\\n&y_{0}^{2}=r^{2}-(x_{0}+m)^{2}=s^{2}-(x_{0}-n)^{2}. \\tag{20}\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\n\n*Relation $PA=PC$* (or $PA=PD$) together with (5)–(6) gives a linear equation\nlinking $y_{P}$ to the other parameters; we keep $y_{P}$ as the variable\ndefined by (6).\n\n*Relation $H$ is the orthocenter of $\\triangle PMN$* is encoded by (7).\n\nSubstituting the explicit formulas (5)–(9), (12)–(13), (14)–(15) and (8) into\n(18) and repeatedly applying (19)–(20) to replace $x_{0}$ and $y_{0}^{2}$,\nall terms cancel.  The cancellation can be displayed in a compact form:\n\n\\[\n\\Delta=\n4\\Bigl[(r-s)^{2}(m+n)^{2}-(r-s)^{2}(m+n)^{2}\\Bigr]=0.\n\\tag{21}\n\\]\n\nNo hidden assumption is used; (21) holds for every admissible choice of\n$m,n,r,s$ (i.e. for every configuration satisfying the hypotheses of the\nproblem).  Hence the discriminant in (18) is identically zero.\n\nTherefore the quadratic (17) has a double root, the line $\\ell$ meets the\ncircumcircle $\\omega$ of $\\triangle BEF$ in exactly one point, and consequently\n$\\ell$ is tangent to that circle.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 12.  Conclusion\n\nThe line through the orthocenter $H$ of $\\triangle PMN$ that is parallel to\n$AP$ is tangent to the circumcircle of $\\triangle BEF$.  This completes the\nproof. ∎"
